WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.173
Allow 4 minutes.

00:00:01.413 --> 00:00:07.109
Prior experience is useful evidence, but familiar habits can conflict with a changed workflow.

00:00:07.349 --> 00:00:13.323
Carnegie Mellon’s learning principles describe prior knowledge as potentially helpful or obstructive.

00:00:13.563 --> 00:00:18.085
A designer needs to uncover both useful knowledge and inaccurate assumptions.

00:00:18.775 --> 00:00:28.059
Worked example: a veteran agent says, “I put the owner’s name in the notes, as we always have.” In the fictional new process, routing reads the Owner field;

00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:29.975
notes do not trigger assignment.

00:00:30.215 --> 00:00:33.223
Start by asking the agent to show the old workflow.

00:00:33.463 --> 00:00:37.325
Identify what remains valid, such as a concise issue summary.

00:00:37.565 --> 00:00:41.426
Then contrast two handoffs with the same notes but different Owner fields.

00:00:41.666 --> 00:00:45.207
Have the learner predict which will route correctly and explain why.

00:00:45.897 --> 00:00:50.761
Avoid framing the veteran as resistant or assuming tenure equals mastery.

00:00:51.001 --> 00:00:54.223
The old behavior may have been correct under the previous system.

00:00:54.463 --> 00:00:59.988
Feedback can acknowledge the reasoning while correcting the action: “Your summary makes the issue clear.

00:01:00.228 --> 00:01:04.772
Routing now uses the Owner field, so select the owner there before submitting.”

00:01:05.462 --> 00:01:12.481
Pause for 30 seconds: identify one thing that should stay the same and one thing that must change in a process you know.

00:01:12.721 --> 00:01:17.329
If using Harbor, preserve the issue summary and change how the owner is assigned.

00:01:18.019 --> 00:01:19.107
Now it's your turn.

00:01:19.347 --> 00:01:23.528
An experienced agent’s demonstration reveals an outdated shortcut.

00:01:23.768 --> 00:01:25.389
Choose the strongest response.

00:01:25.629 --> 00:01:28.744
Pause the video and make your choice in the activity below.

